\par\par \b \uc2 Thai Baht Ends Up On Equity Inflows; Long Bonds Flat  \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2005\par 13:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended firmer against the U.S. dollar in Asian spot trade Thursday on equities-linked inflows and strength in the Japanese yen, a regional trendsetter, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The baht finished off its high, however, on late intraday weakness in the Singapore dollar and on dollar buying by local importers, they said.  \par \par \uc2 Long-term government bonds ended mostly unchanged, as sharp recent declines in prices finally attracted some demand from big funds and insurance companies.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar finished at THB40.905, versus THB40.965 at the Asian close Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at THB40.855 and fell to an intraday low of THB40.800 as offshore players chased the baht for portfolio investment, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been persistent net buyers of Thai shares since July 25.  \par \par \uc2 However, some players bought the dollar back after it rebounded from a three-month low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on fears of equities-related outflows after a selloff in the Singapore stock market.  \par \par \uc2 "Some (Thai) importers also entered the market to buy the dollar in late trade, fearing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would shoot up significantly during the long weekend," a dealer at a major local bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Thai financial markets will be closed Friday to mark the queen's birthday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expected the dollar to trade over the long weekend in a range between THB40.80 and THB41.10, barring any excessive volatility in other major regional units. [ 11-08-05 1112GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Thai long-term government bond bid yields ended mostly unchanged, as big funds and insurance companies returned to buy long bonds after sharp increases in the last two weeks brought yields to attractive levels, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Yields are likely to continue rising gradually in the coming weeks due to the rising interest-rate trend, but the market will likely be calmer next week as renewed demand restrains the curve, they said.  \par \par \uc2 The Thai Bond Dealing Center said the average bid on the government bond due August 2008 finished up one basis point at 4.31%. Dealers said the March 2012 bid was flat at 4.83%, and the August 2019 bid was unchanged at 5.25%.  \par \par \uc2 The most-active overnight interbank rate was expected to have remained unchanged from Wednesday's most-active level of 2.70%, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Quotes for most term interest rates rose slightly, under pressure from the rising trend in offshore and onshore interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Market     Term            Thursday               Wednesday \par Interbank  Overnight/ \par            Most-Active     2.70000% (Estimate)    2.70000% \par Repurchase Overnight/Close 2.71875% (Actual)      2.71875% \par            14-Day/Close    2.75000%               2.75000% \par Bibor      3-Month/11 am   3.20417%               3.19639% \par            6-Month/11 am   3.45208%               3.45319% \par THB Fix    6-Month/11 am   3.65600%               3.64283% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Fox and Phisanu Phromchanya, Dow Jones Newswires; 662 266 0744; djnews.bangkok@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Paul Baylis [ 11-08-05 1123GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1028 1861  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050811e18b000ki\par }\page {\