\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Thai Baht Ends Up On Equity Inflows; Long Bonds Flat  \b0\par\par\uc2 266 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2005\par 14:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK (Dow Jones)--The Thai baht ended firmer against the U.S. dollar in Asian spot trade Thursday on equities-linked inflows and strength in the Japanese yen, a regional trendsetter, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The baht finished off its high, however, on late intraday weakness in the Singapore dollar and on dollar buying by local importers, they said.  \par \par \uc2 Long-term government bonds ended mostly unchanged, as sharp recent declines in prices finally attracted some demand from big funds and insurance companies.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar finished at THB40.905, versus THB40.965 at the Asian close Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at THB40.855 and fell to an intraday low of THB40.800 as offshore players chased the baht for portfolio investment, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign investors have been persistent net buyers of Thai shares since July 25.  \par \par \uc2 However, some players bought the dollar back after it rebounded from a three-month low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on fears of equities-related outflows after a selloff in the Singapore stock market.  \par \par \uc2 'Some (Thai) importers also entered the market to buy the dollar in late trade, fearing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would shoot up significantly during the long weekend,' a dealer at a major local bank said.  \par \par \uc2 Thai financial markets will be closed Friday to mark the queen's birthday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expected the dollar to trade over the long weekend in a range between THB40.80 and THB41.10, barring any excessive volatility in other major regional units.  \par \par \uc2 -0-  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050811e18b007sm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
