\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Surges To One-Week High Vs US Dlr Late  \b0\par\par\uc2 441 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 August 2005\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar surged to a fresh one-week high against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Tuesday, bolstered by speculation that Malaysian authorities may be letting the ringgit rise against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds weakened along the curve, in line with Monday's drop in U.S. Treasurys prices.  \par \par \uc2 At 0637 GMT, the U.S. dollar was S$1.6568, down significantly from S$1.6611 late Monday. It traded between S$1.6566 and S$1.6618 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid to S$1.6566, its lowest level since July 22, dragged by speculator-led selling of U.S. dollars for other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said speculators apparently used the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's break below MYR3.7500, where Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely rumored to have placed U.S. dollar bids, as an excuse to sell the U.S. dollar against the Singaporean and some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The market is following the ringgit," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer, adding that there was no sign of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp rise.  \par \par \uc2 The apparent lack of U.S. dollar bids from the Malaysian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the psychologically important MYR3.7500 level spurred market talk that Bank Negara Malaysia may be allowing a gradual appreciation of the ringgit in the face of broad-based U.S. dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0637 GMT, the U.S. unit was at MYR3.7460, down from MYR3.7500 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Philip Wee, senior market strategist at DBS Bank, said Tuesday's rally of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  shows that the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is technically positioning for a weaker U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the U.S. dollar's break below the S$1.6600 support in the session, the U.S. unit is likely to find strong technical support at S$1.6530, Wee said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       1.8125%    1.8125% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.04%      2.03% \par    10-year bond yield   2.72%      2.68% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.20%      2.18% \par    10-year swap offer   2.91%      2.88% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 71 BPs     70 BPs \par  \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui and Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Mary de Wet [ 02-08-05 0656GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050802e18200091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
