\par\par \b \uc2 = UPDATE: Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Pare Gains, Stks Mixed On Yuan   \b0\par\par\uc2 861 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jul 22, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --   \par \par \uc2 A number of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday gave back a portion of their sharp gains that followed China's revaluation of the yuan against the U.S. dollar late Thursday, while regional stock markets were mixed and local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds recovered from an initial slump.   \par \par \uc2 Reaction in oil and commodities markets was mostly muted.   \par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded quickly to China's decision to scrap the yuan's decade-old peg to the dollar and shift to an undisclosed basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 The yen surged against the dollar, with the U.S. unit tumbling more than two yen to a low of Y109.87 after the announcement. But the dollar bounced back Friday in Tokyo as traders decided to wait and see how China would actually control the yuan under the new system before pushing up the yen further.   \par \par \uc2 At 0920 GMT, the dollar was at Y110.89, more than half a yen back up from Y110.28 late Thursday in New York.   \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar shot up above US$0.7650 from US$0.7600 before China's move on perceptions that Australia's commodity-heavy economy will benefit from the yuan move. It, unlike the yen, continued to climb and was last at US$0.7676.   \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore market, the U.S. dollar fell to S$1.6511 from S$1.6804 in late Asian trading Thursday, but recovered Friday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged companies to step up U.S. dollar buying, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Speculative buying of the Singapore dollar was also helped by Malaysia's decision to abandon its peg to the dollar and move to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket regime.   \par \par \uc2 Despite Malaysia's policy change, the U.S. dollar didn't weaken much against the ringgit and was last quoted at MYR3.7775.   \par \par \uc2 Chua Hak Bin, an economist at Singapore's DBS Bank, said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may rally more, led by the Japanese yen and the Taiwan dollar, because of their huge trade with China.   \par \par \uc2 But he said the Chinese appreciation is smaller than expected, which will prompt market players to speculate on more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation in the months ahead.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nikkei Slumps On Fears For Exporters\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia-Pacific stock markets were mixed.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese shares led the region's gainers. Airline, property and paper-related companies in the mainland did especially well.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks both A and B shares in China, ended up 2.5% at 1046.32. The Shenzhen Composite Index ended up 2.2% to 245.57.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan, which was hurt by concerns that a stronger yen would cut into exporter's profits, led the region's decliners.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended down 0.8% at 11695.05. Japanese exporters were susceptible to profit-taking following their recent run-up, which were driven by hopes that their earnings would get a boost from a weaker Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reform offered "a perfect incentive" for selling, as Tokyo stocks were due for a correction after their near-continuous gains since May, said Takashi Miyazaki, senior strategist at UFJ Partners Asset Management.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taiwan's Weighted Price Index ended down 0.2% at 6380.73. Technology shares accounted for 72% of turnover, down from their more usual level recently of around 80%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, ended down 0.04% at 1074.22.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds Claw Back Losses\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds fell early Friday in a knee-jerk response to the yuan revaluation, but recovered quickly in anticipation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation in the region staves off rate hikes.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stronger Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are also ultimately likely to ease the debt burden for those countries and companies - like the Philippines, Indonesia, Thailand and China - that have local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  revenues and large U.S. dollar-denominated liabilities.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The oil futures market reacted largely coolly to China's move.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Toward the end of the Asian trading day, with European markets opening for business, Nymex benchmark light sweet crude for September delivery was trading at $57.40 a barrel, up 27 cents from the New York floor trade close.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan revaluation had triggered a brief oil price rally as a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  makes imports of dollar-priced oil cheaper for China.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reaction in commodities markets was muted, apart from trading in palm oil, as the size of the yuan revaluation was smaller than expected. Palm oil prices fell on fears that a de-pegged Malaysian ringgit will make it less competitive in the international market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the long term, prices of rubber, iron ore, grains, copper and gold could rise as the yuan's revaluation has made it cheaper for China to import such commodities, analysts said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is the world's largest buyer of soybeans as also natural rubber, iron ore and several other commodities. It also buys several other commodities such as gold in large quantities.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dow Jones Newswires, Singapore, 65-6415-4105   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Jenny Paris and Iain McDonald   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 07-22-05 0528ET   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050722e17m00267\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
