\par\par \b \uc2 = Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Pare Gains, Stks Mixed After Yuan Shift  \b0\par\par\uc2 794 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 22:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Jul 22, 2005 (ODC via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave back some of their sharp gains Friday after China revalued the yuan against the U.S. dollar late Thursday, while regional stock markets were mixed and local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds recovered from an initial slump.  \par \par \uc2 Reaction in oil and commodities markets was muted.  \par \par \uc2 Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded quickly to China's decision to scrap the yuan's decade-old peg to the dollar and shift to an undisclosed basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 The yen surged against the dollar, with the U.S. unit tumbling more than two yen to a low of Y109.87 after the announcement. But the dollar bounced back Friday in Tokyo as traders decided to wait and see how China would actually control the yuan under the new system before pushing up the yen further.  \par \par \uc2 At 0532 GMT, the dollar was at Y111.05, almost a full yen back up from Y110.28 late Thursday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar shot up above US$0.7650 from US$0.7600 before China's move on perceptions that Australia's commodity-heavy economy will benefit from the yuan move. It was last at US$0.7647.  \par \par \uc2 In the Singapore market, the U.S. dollar fell to S$1.6511 from S$1.6804 in late Asian trading Thursday, but has since partly recovered on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  encouraged companies to step up U.S. dollar buying, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculative buying of the Singapore dollar was also helped by Malaysia's decision to abandon its peg to the dollar and move to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket regime.  \par \par \uc2 Despite Malaysia's policy change, the U.S. dollar didn't weaken much against the ringgit and was last quoted at MYR3.7825.  \par \par \uc2 Chua Hak Bin, an economist at Singapore's DBS Bank, said Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may rally more, led by the Japanese yen and the Taiwan dollar, because of their huge trade with China.  \par \par \uc2 But he said the Chinese appreciation is smaller than expected, which will prompt market players to speculate on more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation in the months ahead.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nikkei Slumps On Fears For Exporters\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asia-Pacific stock markets are mixed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average is down around 0.8% at 11689.91. Analysts believe the yuan revaluation is generally positive for Japanese shares because it reduces trade tensions between Tokyo and Beijing. But some said a stronger yen may squeeze some Japanese exporters. Nikkei's weakness so far Friday is likely due to "sell-on-news" mentality, as the yuan revaluation had long been anticipated, analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's stock markets are rising, with airline stocks surging as they are expected to benefit from a strong yuan regime because many of those companies have outstanding U.S. dollar debt, which will be easier to service.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index, which tracks both A and B shares, rose 0.6% by mid-morning.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Korea Composite Stock Price Index, or Kospi, is down 0.1%, Australia's benchmark S&P/ASX 200 is up 0.3%, and Singapore's Straits Times Index is up around 0.3%. Taiwan's Weighted Price Index is down around 0.3%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds Claw Back Losses\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bonds fell early Friday in a kneejerk response to the yuan revaluation, but recovered quickly in anticipation that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciation in the region staves off rate hikes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stronger Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are also ultimately likely to help those countries and companies - like the Philippines, Indonesia, Thailand and China - which have local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  revenues and large U.S. dollar-denominated liabilities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The oil futures market reacted largely coolly to China's move.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 By the middle of the Asia trading day, and shortly before European markets got going, Nymex benchmark light sweet crude for September delivery was trading at $57.42 a barrel, up 29 cents from the New York floor trade close.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan revaluation had triggered a brief oil price rally as a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  makes imports of dollar-priced oil cheaper for China.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reaction in commodities markets was muted as China's decision to revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by 2% to CNY8.11 to the U.S. dollar was a smaller shift than expected. However, prices of commodities could rise in the long run as the revaluation has made it cheaper for China to import the goods.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China is the world's largest buyer of soybeans as also natural rubber, iron ore and several other commodities. It also buys several other commodities such as gold in large quantities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dow Jones Newswires, Singapore, 65-6415-4105  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Jenny Paris  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 07-22-05 0139ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050722e17m001zv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
