\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-MAS will  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  if Sing dollar nears policy band  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mia Shanley  \par \uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juli 2005\par 05:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, background)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , July 22 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar nears its policy band and reiterated its commitment to a policy of gradual and modest appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Ong Chong Tee, deputy managing director of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), said the Singapore dollar <SGD=> was currently trading at the top half of its band.  \par \par \uc2 "We will come into the market when the Singapore dollar looks to reach the policy band," Ong told a news conference where the bank presented its 2004/05 annual report.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said China's revaluation of its yuan on Thursday would not have a major impact on the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Our policy has allowed us to accommodate the changes that have taken place. We see no need to change the regime," said Heng Swee Keat, MAS managing director.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS conducts monetary policy by steering the Singapore dollar in a band against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than setting an interest rate as many central banks do.  \par \par \uc2 The band and the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are kept secret.  \par \par \uc2 The city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  added that persistently high oil prices were posing an upside risk to external inflation.  \par \par \uc2 GROWTH TO SLOW  \par \par \uc2 On the economic front, the bank said in its annual report that Singapore's growth will ease in 2005, as a slowdown in the electronics sector weighs on the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 "The Singapore economy is poised to descend from its cyclical high in 2004, and is expected to grow between 2.5 percent and 4.5 percent in 2005," the MAS said, reaffirming the government's forecast in May.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said it expected inflation in 2006 to rise to 1 to 2 percent from zero to 1.0 percent in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the Singapore dollar strengthened by about two percent to 1.6498 against the U.S. dollar compared to Wednesday's close after China and Malaysia revalued their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But after rallying to a near two-month high, the Singapore dollar eased back to 1.6574 within half an hour, which traders said was due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The bank said on Friday that Thursday's two percent move in the Singapore dollar was "not considered too volatile".  \par \par \uc2 The monetary policy will be reviewed again in October, the bank said. ($1=1.686 Singapore Dollar)  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-SINGAPORE-MAS (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|Z|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050722e17m00090\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
