\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  to rise on China move, for a while  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 775 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juli 2005\par 16:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 21 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply on Thursday after China and Malaysia announced they were abandoning \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs with the dollar and analysts said they would rise further, at least, in the next few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 But speculative money that had flowed into Asia betting on a yuan and ringgit revaluation could start to leave Asia, undermining Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar in the longer term, they said.  \par \par \uc2 "People are going to get out of this trade at this point," said Tim Condon, head of Asian research at ING. "The incentive to hang around for another big move is diminished considerably."  \par \par \uc2 Asia's traditionally \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  central banks were quick to warn markets against pushing regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up too sharply.  \par \par \uc2 Japan and South Korea issued such warnings, while authorities in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  were suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb strength in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar.  \par \par \uc2 China said it was scrapping the yuan's dollar peg, revaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 2.1 percent to 8.11 per dollar, and tying it to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China's major trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 Shortly after that, Malaysia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was dropping its ringgit-dollar peg and adopting a managed float.  \par \par \uc2 The announcements came after Asian markets had closed trading for the day.  \par \par \uc2 Still, traders in Singapore and London pushed the Singapore dollar <SGD=> up 2.2 percent from the day's low to a two-month high of 1.6498 per U.S. dollar. The Thai baht <THB=> moved to a 3-week high after China's move.  \par \par \uc2 "It will have a significant impact. We expect dollar/won to hit our forecast of 990 by the end of September, and expect dollar/Taiwan to hit 29," said Claudio Piron, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JPMorgan in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 "Hong Kong will fly as well but because it has a peg it will be reflected in the property and stock markets," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The Korean won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> was traded around 1,025 per dollar in thin offshore dealings late on Thursday, up 1.5 percent from the day's low, while the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> was quoted around 31.65, up a percent from the day's trough.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan was also quoted in offshore non-deliverable forwards <CNY=CFXS> <CNYNDF=> at 7.9 per dollar at the end of one year, reflecting the market view that the managed float will result in a steady appreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Piron said JPMorgan expected a 7 percent appreciation in the yuan by the end of the year, and that would keep other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure to rise, even if speculative money flowed out of the region.  \par \par \uc2 HOT MONEY  \par \par \uc2 Investors had poured billions of dollars into Asia in the past three years betting that China would revalue the yuan, taking the competitive heat off its neighbours in export markets.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Singapore dollar, won and Taiwan dollar, the most popular proxies used until now to bet on a yuan revaluation will be the fastest to rise over the next few days.  \par \par \uc2 While most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  compete with China in global export markets, China is also a big buyer of Asian exports. Hong Kong and Taiwan sell about 40 percent of their exports to China.  \par \par \uc2 "Once the dust settles from this knee-jerk move, the dollar will resume its strengthening trend and these gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prove short-lived, I suspect," Condon said.  \par \par \uc2 According to Morgan Stanley's economist Andy Xie, about $700 billion in speculative money is parked in Asia -- three times the amount that left the region in the Asian financial crisis and far higher than Asia's $200 billion annual trade surplus.  \par \par \uc2 Xie estimates half that money is invested in China, with another 25 percent in Japan and the rest in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In a report issued earlier this month, Xie said China should not revalue the yuan and should tighten policy to prevent a flight of capital.  \par \par \uc2 Simon Flint, head of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch, said there was no such danger.  \par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might limit the yuan's moves initially, but the country's mounting trade surplus and foreign fund inflows will force the yuan higher, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Hot money flows stay and intensify. We think the speculative game carries on and the pressure will remain on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue to appreciate," he said.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|U|NAT|Z|SI|IF|PHD|SNS|SNI|J|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050721e17l001so\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
