\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Down Late; Near 9-Month Low On Yen Fall  \b0\par\par\uc2 401 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2005\par 10:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar fell to nearly a nine-month low against the U.S. dollar in late Asian trade Friday, dragged down by a surge of the U.S. unit against the Japanese yen following a rate hike by the Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices rose slightly after overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. unit traded between S$1.6823 and S$1.6908, its highest level since S$1.6906 on Oct. 8. At 0722 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.6894, up from S$1.6820 late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 'Although there's a long weekend in the U.S., the market went a bit wild after the Japanese yen fell,' said a dealer at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said they were expecting some consolidation Friday ahead of the weekend and the July 4 holiday in the U.S. However, selling in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  accelerated after the U.S. dollar rose to a near-nine-month high of Y111.13.  \par \par \uc2 Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are at multi-month lows against the U.S. dollar due to high oil prices, weakening speculation of a yuan revaluation, and rising U.S. interest rates. The Federal Reserve, as expected, on Thursday raised its key target rate to 3.25% from 3%.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   United Overseas Bank said in a research report the U.S. dollar should face resistance above S$1.6900 because market players will be cautious of any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   The Singapore dollar has support because the MAS doesn't appear to be in the mood of abandoning its policy of modest and gradual appreciation of the local dollar against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the next few months, UOB said. \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       1.813%     2.063% \par    Two-year bond yield  1.94%      1.95% \par    10-year bond yield   2.61%      2.62% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.11%      2.13% \par    10-year swap offer   2.73%      2.78% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 62 BPs     65 BPs \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com -Edited by Lena Lee -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050701e1710060p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
