\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Steadier after falling on euro   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2005\par 03:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 1 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  steadied on Wednesday after falling for two days, in line with the euro's moves, but markets braced for further weakness on account of reduced expectations for a Chinese yuan revaluation.   \par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> steadied briefly in Asian trade but soon extended a slide that began on Monday to hit a 7-1/2-month low below $1.2294 in European trading.   \par \par \uc2 The steep euro drop was triggered by political and economic concerns after France's rejection in a referendum of a proposed European Union constitution, and the euro was seen staying weak on Wednesday as the Dutch began voting in a similar referendum.   \par \par \uc2 In Asia, the Korean won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> and the Thai baht <THB=> were the biggest losers.   \par \par \uc2 The won came within striking distance of a 100-day moving average around 1,013 a dollar, taking its decline this week to 1.2 percent, before paring losses.   \par \par \uc2 The Thai baht breached key support at 40.70 per dollar. The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell to 1.6710, its lowest level against the dollar since October.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said the dollar's euro-driven rally and the paring of bets on a change in policy for China's virtually pegged yuan had undermined regionals. Banks had to hurriedly cover their bullish Asian positions, they said.   \par \par \uc2 China decided on Monday to scrap export tariffs on 81 textile products in a tit-for-tat move after the United States and Europe curbed Chinese textile exports.   \par \par \uc2 "One theme that continued to permeate through Asia was shorts in dollar-Asia," Westpac Bank said in a note.   \par \par \uc2 "The very aggressive push back from China on trade sanctions suggests that they have little interest in responding to foreign pressure," Westpac said   \par \par \uc2 The market still has a lot of positions betting on a yuan revaluation, so there was potential for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be sold further, it said.   \par \par \uc2 Traders suspected several regional central banks had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "It seems the MAS (\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has been trying to contain the Sing dollar's fall but the BOK (Bank of Korea) is leaning into the move today," a trader said, referring to talk that the South Korean authorities had bought dollars to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 BUCKING THE BEARS   \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> and the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> stood firm, the latter still underpinned by strong equity flows as players adjust positions after Taiwan's reweighting in the MSCI Emerging Markets Index on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "Of the top 3 performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the year -- peso, won and Taiwan dollar -- the weakest link of them all is won," said said Claudio Piron, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 "And the most robust one is Taiwan because you still have some combination of domestic and external performance complementing one another."   \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley Capital International Inc., a global stock index compiler, said last year it would raise the weighting of Taiwan stocks in its emerging markets index.   \par \par \uc2 Taiwan weightings in the index were raised in 2 stages. The second phase took effect on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have been pumping money into Taiwan in anticipation of the reweighting, although they turned net sellers on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Data from Nomura International shows overseas investors bought a net $13.9 billion of Taiwan equities in the 250 days to May 29.   \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar was quoted around 31.38/39 per dollar on Wednesday and has shed about a quarter of a percent this week.   \par \par \uc2 "Due to the Taiwan MSCI reweighting, we actually saw only 2 days of net foreign equity selling last month," Piron said.   \par \par \uc2 "One should think that most of the participants have moved now but there should still be some support coming from there." \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0825 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.49         108.00       -0.45\par  Sing dlr        1.6679         1.6634       -0.27\par  Taiwan dlr      31.390         31.363       -0.09\par  Korean won     1010.60        1007.70       -0.29\par  Baht             40.70          40.57       -0.32\par  Peso             54.56          54.52       -0.07\par  Rupiah         9535.00        9505.00       -0.31\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.49         102.45       -5.57\par  Sing dlr        1.6679         1.6310       -2.21\par  Taiwan dlr      31.390         31.917       +1.68\par  Korean won     1010.60        1035.10       +2.42\par  Baht             40.70          38.85       -4.55\par  Peso             54.56          56.28       +3.15\par  Rupiah         9535.00        9270.00       -2.78\par  Rupee            43.75          43.47       -0.64\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|Z|IF|PHD|J|SNS|SNI|