\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Won, baht weaker, Taiwan dlr stands firm   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan   \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juni 2005\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, June 1 (Reuters) - Continuing weakness in the euro weighed on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, taking the Thai baht to its lowest since November and pressing the Singapore dollar and Korean won down further.   \par \par \uc2 The euro <EUR=> extended a slide that began on Monday to hit a fresh seven-month low in New York.   \par \par \uc2 The steep euro drop was triggered by political and economic concerns after France's rejection of a proposed European Union constitution, and the euro was seen staying weak ahead of a Dutch referendum on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell to 1.6676 per dollar in early deals, down half a percent from last week's close.   \par \par \uc2 The baht <THB=>, also hurt by data showing a deteriorating current account, was traded around 40.70 a dollar and has shed one percent this week.   \par \par \uc2 The Korean won <KRW=> <KRW=KFTC> was a hair's breadth away from key support of a 100-day moving average around 1,013 a dollar. The won has lost 1.2 percent this week.   \par \par \uc2 "There is some nervousness in the market. Long term averages and technical supports on the majors have all broken through," said Claudio Piron, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the dollar's rise had led to stop-losses being triggered and a scramble for dollars in Asia. They suspected several regional central banks had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "It seems the MAS (\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has been trying to contain the Sing dollar's fall but the BOK (Bank of Korea) is leaning into the move today," a trader said, referring to talk that the South Korean authorities had bought dollars to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 BUCKING THE BEARS   \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso <PHP=> and the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> stood firm, the latter still underpinned by strong equity flows as players adjust positions after Taiwan's reweighting in the MSCI Emerging Markets Index on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 "Of the top 3 performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the year -- peso, won and Taiwan dollar -- the weakest link of them all is won," said JP Morgan's Piron.   \par \par \uc2 "And the most robust one is Taiwan because you still have some combination of domestic and external performance complementing one another."   \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley Capital International Inc., a global stock index compiler, said last year it would raise the weighting of Taiwan stocks in its emerging markets index.   \par \par \uc2 Taiwan weightings in the index was raised in 2 stages. The second phase took effect on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have been pumping money into Taiwan in anticipation of the reweighting. Data Nomura International shows overseas investors bought a net $13.9 billion of Taiwan equities in the 250 days to May 29.   \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar was quoted around 31.38/39 per dollar on Wednesday and has shed about a quarter of a percent this week.   \par \par \uc2 "Due to the Taiwan MSCI reweighting, we actually saw only 2 days of net foreign equity selling last month," Piron said.   \par \par \uc2 "One should think that most of the participants have moved now but there should still be some support coming from there."   \par \par \uc2 Besides, foreign investors were still underweight against the benchmark index on Taiwan and the equity market was benefitting from a flow of funds from the commodity sectors back into technology stocks, he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0347 GMT    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.26         108.00       -0.24\par  Sing dlr        1.6653         1.6634       -0.11\par  Taiwan dlr      31.388         31.363       -0.08\par  Korean won     1011.10        1007.70       -0.34\par  Baht             40.68          40.57       -0.26\par  Peso             54.51          54.52       +0.02\par  Rupiah         9537.00        9505.00       -0.34\par  Change on year\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       108.26         102.45       -5.37\par  Sing dlr        1.6653         1.6310       -2.06\par  Taiwan dlr      31.388         31.917       +1.69\par  Korean won     1011.10        1035.10       +2.37\par  Baht             40.68          38.85       -4.49\par  Peso             54.51          56.28       +3.25\par  Rupiah         9537.00        9270.00       -2.80\par  Rupee            43.70          43.47       -0.51\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|SI|Z|IF|PHD|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050601e1610006e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
