\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:HK, Asia Markets Stay Strong After HKD Peg Change  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Torchia  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 721 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2005\par 10:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock prices across East Asia stayed strong Thursday despite Hong Kong's move to deter the inflows of speculative "hot money" which have been buoying markets across the region.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong traders said that after an initial outflow of funds from the territory on Wednesday night, there was no sign of major outflows on Thursday, and there were even some fresh inflows of foreign money into the stock market. The Hang Seng index ended 0.5% higher at 13698.93 points, confounding fears that it would slide.  \par \par \uc2 "We saw some of these speculators closing positions yesterday and going away, and now they are back again," said Francis Lun at Fulbright Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Asia, Hong Kong's action only served to increase speculation that China would soon change its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, allowing the yuan to appreciate against the U.S. dollar. Taiwan was one market buoyed by such hopes; its benchmark stock index closed up 1.4% at 5970.71, also helped by Wall Street's strong performance Wednesday, while the Taiwan dollar firmed to 31.300 against the U.S. dollar from its previous close of 31.383.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday evening, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was introducing a ceiling for the Hong Kong dollar against the U.S. dollar to discourage its use as a trading proxy for speculation about a revaluation of the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 The surprise announcement raised the possibility that other economies experiencing big inflows of funds speculating on the yuan - China itself, as well as Malaysia and to a lesser extent Singapore, Taiwan and South Korea - could take similar action. Any reversal of the flood of hot money into Asia could deal a major short-term blow to the region's markets.  \par \par \uc2 But on Thursday morning, most investors were clearly betting that because China has sovereignty over Hong Kong, the HKMA may have been informed of an imminent yuan revaluation and was making preparations to keep the Hong Kong dollar stable at that time.  \par \par \uc2 RBC Capital Markets said the HKMA's move was part of a series of events that "will eventually lead to a yuan revalue in the coming weeks". It predicts China will move from a de facto yuan peg against the U.S. dollar to a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , producing a rise of the yuan against the dollar of between 3% and 5%.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts, however, think that theory is wrong, noting that Chinese leaders have in recent days strongly indicated they won't be bullied by foreign pressure into rushing a yuan revaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of America's Robert Sinche said he didn't believe  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong's move was linked to knowledge of any imminent yuan policy change. Instead, he argued, it "merely reflects the HKMA's interest in maintaining stable conditions in local markets. With large on-again, off-again speculative capital inflows since 2003... the HKMA began to consider the need for a two-sided commitment."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch, while noting that a yuan revaluation was only a matter of time, said it doubted the HKMA would have had advance knowledge of a yuan revaluation. "Instead, we think HKD rates are too low for official comfort (and the) pace of the recent increases in property prices and CPI inflation too high for official comfort."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And although some East Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  benefited Thursday from hopes they would attract speculative funds leaving the Hong Kong dollar, the region's central banks may attempt to prevent that.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore is unlikely to allow increased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bets using the Singapore dollar as a proxy for revaluations of the yuan or the Malaysian ringgit, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's UOB said. Traders in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected the MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  early Thursday to erase some of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar's gains on Wednesday night.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas strategist Naomi Fink said Hong Kong's move probably wouldn't have much further impact on Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the short term - but that it did imply speculative flows into Asia could be curtailed in the medium term. That was a possibility that Asian markets on Thursday appeared to ignore.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      -By Andrew Torchia; (65) 6415-4013; andrew.torchia@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 19-05-05 0844GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050519e15j000gs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
