\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Surges To 5-Week High Vs US Dollar Late  \b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 April 2005\par 09:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar surged to a five-week high against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Friday, boosted by increased speculation that the Chinese authorities might revalue the yuan during week-long holidays in China next week.  \par \par \uc2 Prices on Singapore government bonds edged up in the wake of higher U.S. Treasury prices late on Thursday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0715 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6423, down from S$1.6467 late Thursday. It traded between S$1.6326 and S$1.6477 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After consolidating around the S$1.6470 level in the morning, the U.S. dollar soon plunged to as low as S$1.6326, its lowest since March 22, on rising expectations that China could move to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 Fueling these expectations was a surprisingly sharp fall in the U.S. dollar to as low as CNY8.2700 from Thursday's close of CNY8.2765, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Although the spot dollar-yuan stayed within the official daily trading band of 0.3% on either side of CNY8.277, analysts said the unexpected plunge in the dollar spooked market participants into selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the Singapore dollar and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and heightened speculation that China may be preparing to adopt a freer exchange rate sooner rather than later.  \par \par \uc2 This speculation arose despite China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman saying Friday that there is no plan to adjust yuan policy during the Labor Day holiday.  \par \par \uc2 "As far as we know, there's no adjustment expected in the yuan exchange rate," Bai Li, spokesman for the People's Bank of China, told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Bai made the comment in response to a question on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would revalue the yuan during the week-long holiday starting May 1.  \par \par \uc2 Given the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tumble against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the intraday low of S$1.6326, lifting the U.S. unit back up to near the mid-S$1.6400s, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remained jittery, with some players saying the U.S. dollar could retest S$1.6326 if U.S. economic data to be released later in the global session surprise on the downside.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. first-quarter employment cost index, March personal income and March personal spending data will be released at 1230 GMT. These data will be followed by the final University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index for April at 1345 GMT and the April Chicago purchasing manager index at 1400 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       1.8750%    2.0625% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.10%      2.11% \par    10-year bond yield   2.89%      2.90% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.21%      2.26% \par    10-year swap offer   3.08%      3.14% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 87 BPs     88 BPs \par  \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web s