\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Rises To 1-Wk High Late; Yuan Revalue Talk  \b0\par\par\uc2 640 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2005\par 09:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose to a one-week high against its U.S. counterpart late Monday in Asia, bolstered by market speculation of a near-term revaluation in the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bond prices were slightly higher across the board, in line with strength in U.S. Treasurys on Friday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6474, down from S$1.6506 late Friday. It traded between S$1.6436 and S$1.6478 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, market participants sold the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to as low as S$1.6436, its lowest since April 14. The selling was mainly because of bullish sentiment toward the Singapore dollar on expectations that China would move to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime sooner rather than later, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Although concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a sharp rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar spurred light U.S.-dollar buybacks that lifted the U.S. unit to around the S$1.6480 level in the morning, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to be weighed down against the local dollar by sell-on-rally interest for the rest of the session.  \par \par \uc2 Fueling the yuan revaluation speculation were fresh remarks from Chinese officials over the weekend. A Chinese regulator said Sunday that China may speed up preparations for changes to its exchange rate regime, though emphasized that any such reforms will continue to be driven by domestic priorities and not criticism from its trading partners.  \par \par \uc2 "I agree that the economy is growing well, and probably it's time for China to consider to take the change. In general terms, a more flexible exchange rate is beneficial to the economy," said Wei Benhua, deputy director of the State Administration of \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We can positively and prudently accelerate reform of the renminbi exchange rate regime," he said.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, however, Wei said "there is no timetable" for any change to the renminbi regime. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the yuan, is effectively pegged around 8.28 to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Wei's remarks came on the heels of comments made Saturday by the governor of the People's Bank of China, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We are making some preparations," Zhou Xiaochuan said, pointing specifically to ongoing reforms in the domestic banking system.  \par \par \uc2 However, he also said it was a gradual process and that "we have our own sequencing of reforms according to the Chinese situation."  \par \par \uc2 From a technical analysis perspective, the repeated failure of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise above resistance at S$1.6515-S$1.6520 has increased the risk of a further fall in the short term, United Overseas Bank said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 "Expect the strong support at S$1.6400 to be tested, but currently it is unclear whether a sustained down-move is possible," UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 UOB also said a rebound of the U.S. dollar above resistance at S$1.6515 would indicate that the downside risk has diminished.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \