\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Up Late On Speculation Over China  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Move  \b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2005\par 09:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar rose late Wednesday in Asia, as growing speculation that China may shift to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime outweighed rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Yields on \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds fell more in the long end than in other sectors, resulting in curve flattening, in line with Tuesday's flattening in the Treasurys yield curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0700 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6485, down from S$1.6527 late Tuesday. It traded between S$1.6457 and S$1.6504 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the U.S. dollar fell as low as S$1.6457 on speculator-led selling sparked by increasing rhetoric from top U.S. officials such as President George W. Bush and Treasury Secretary John Snow over the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Late Tuesday, both Bush and Snow reiterated recent remarks calling on the Chinese authorities to move now toward a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime.  \par \par \uc2 In a research note, Morgan Stanley said these comments are likely aimed at diffusing efforts by the U.S. Senate to debate protectionist steps. The Senate is considering legislation to impose steep tariffs if China doesn't allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise.  \par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore apparently stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to buy the U.S. dollar around the intraday low of S$1.6457, thereby boosting the U.S. unit as high as S$1.6504 by mid-day, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Later in the session, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then pulled back slightly to the high-S$1.6400s amid thin market conditions, as many players are awaiting the 1230 GMT release of the U.S. consumer price index for March. The CPI for March is expected to show a 0.5% increase. The core index is seen rising 0.2%.  \par \par \uc2 The following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                         Latest     Previous \par    Overnight rate       2.1250%    2.1875% \par    Two-year bond yield  2.07%      2.09% \par    10-year bond yield   2.97%      3.00% \par    Two-year swap offer  2.28%      2.30% \par    10-year swap offer   3.17%      3.16% \par    2-10-yr swap spread: 89 BPs     86 BPs \par  \par    Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hilary Mc Cully [ 20-04-05 0732GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050420e14k0009d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
