\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 2-Singapore MAS maintains monetary policy stance.  \b0\par\par\uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2005\par 02:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, April 12 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), the city-state's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , retained on Tuesday its year-old tightening stance of allowing a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb inflation risks, even as economic growth slows.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS said it would maintain its policy of allowing "a modest and gradual appreciation" of the trade-weighted Singapore dollar, which was adopted last April when Singapore's economy was undergoing rapid economic expansion.  \par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:  \par \par \uc2 - Most economists had expected the MAS to leave its year-long policy of "a modest and gradual appreciation" of the Singapore dollar unchanged because of inflation risks.  \par \par \uc2 - A Reuters poll of 11 analysts take after Monday's unexpectedly weak economic growth data found that more than two-thirds expected the MAS to keep its tighter policy stance.  \par \par \uc2 - The MAS cut its forecast for headline CPI inflation to 0-1 percent for 2005 against previous forecast of 1-2 percent. For 2006, the MAS expects headline CPI inflation of 1-2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 - The MAS expects Singapore's gross domestic product growth to come in at the lower half of the government's forecast range of 3-5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 - MAS said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly in the fourth quarter of 2004 and into early 2005 to moderate excessive upward pressure on the Singapore dollar.  \par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:  \par \par \uc2 SIMON FLINT, HEAD OF EMERGING ASIA \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGIST, MERRILL LYNCH  \par \par \uc2 "This was very much in line with my expectation and to some extent, you can see this in the market place - there has been some fairly modest appreciation by the Sing dollar. But because of the recently, relatively poor GDP numbers and the very low inflation numbers, some people may have thought they would change. The impact of growth will be very minor.  \par \par \uc2 "The things that have interested me so far are two-fold. First of all, their slightly muted outlook for inflation this year and saying between 0 to 1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "Also this mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was used to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar strength which I would interpret as them \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to prevent the speculative pricing of either Chinese or Malaysian regime change on the Sing dollar.  \par \par \uc2 IRENE CHEUNG, HEAD OF ASIA \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  STRATEGY, ABN AMRO BANK  \par \par \uc2 "Probably they are still looking at better numbers for GDP for the rest of the year and despite the worse-than-expected GDP growth for the first quarter of this year. They have lowered their inflation forecast to 0 to 1 percent from 1 to 2 percent and I think that is in line with what we see from the lastest CPI numbers.  \par \par \uc2 "They are having a very, very slight appreciation of the Sing dollar so it won't have a major impact on growth."  \par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEE, TREASURY MARKET STRATEGIST, DBS BANK  \par \par \uc2 "It signals that the economy is seeing a soft patch and not a recession. We are also being consistent with what's happening around us, which is that interest rates are being tightened."  \par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:  \par \par \uc2 - The Singapore dollar was trading at 1.6461 to the U.S. dollar after the announcement, compared with 1.6502 just before 0000 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 - The stock market was not open when the review was announced. The Straits Times Index ended flat at 2,181.58 points on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 LINK  \par \par \uc2 - To view the full report, please go to the Web site of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore at  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mas.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.mas.gov.sg }}}\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:  \par \par \uc2 - The MAS conducts monetary policy by steering the Singapore dollar in a band against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than setting an interest rate as many \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  do.  \par \par \uc2 - The band and the basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are kept secret, though most major financial institutions have developed models to broadly mirror the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's system.  \par \par \uc2 - Singapore's economy grew 8.4 percent in 2004, its fastest in four years, but analysts polled by Reuters have forecast the growth rate would halve to 4.2 percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 - The economy contracted at an annualised 5.8 percent rate in the first quarter, far worse than market expectations.  \par \par \uc2 - The MAS has estimated that consumer prices in 2005 will rise between 1 percent and 2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 - The inflation rate in 2004 hit a 7-year high of 1.7 percent after a rise in a goods-and-services tax and power tariffs.  \par \par \uc2 ($1=1.65 Singapore Dollar).  \par \par \uc2 . (C) Reuters Limited 2005.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050412e14c000e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
