\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Edges Up Late After Broad-Based US Dlr Fall  \b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 08:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar edged higher in very thin trading late Monday in Asia after broad-based U.S. dollar weakness Friday spurred by the second-highest U.S. monthly trade deficit on record.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore government bonds were weaker across the board after U.S. Treasurys ended on a negative note Friday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6210, down from S$1.6218 late Friday. It traded between S$1.6183 and S$1.6211 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 From the start of Monday's Asian session, the U.S. dollar traded with a bearish tone against its Singaporean counterpart, weighed down by negative sentiment fueled by Friday's wider-than-expected U.S. January trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department said Friday that the U.S. deficit in international trade of goods and services grew 4.5% to US$58.27 billion in January from a revised US$55.74 billion trade deficit in December.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. January trade deficit was just shy of the all-time high of US$59.42 billion posted in November, and was larger than Wall Street economists had expected. A Dow Jones-CNBC survey of 18 economists had projected a deficit of US$56.50 billion.  \par \par \uc2 While speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb a sharp rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar put a floor under the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the session, market participants feared that this week's U.S. economic data could send the U.S. dollar back down to last Thursday's six-year intraday low of S$1.6161 in coming sessions.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury International Capital System flow data for January are due Tuesday and the U.S.'s fourth-quarter current account deficit data are scheduled for release Wednesday, both of which could tilt the scales further toward U.S. dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the TICS data are likely to show the extent of foreigners' appetite for U.S. securities, and thereby their willingness to finance the massive U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Latest       Previous \par Overnight rate       1.7500%      1.7500% \par Two-year bond yield  2.07%        2.05% \par 10-year bond yield   3.24%        3.21% \par Two-year swap offer  2.41%        2.38% \par 10-year swap offer   3.49%        3.47% \par 2-10-yr swap spread  108 Bps      109 Bps \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lena Lee  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050314e13e005pm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
