\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Bonds Tumble On Treasurys, Tight MAS Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Mar 09, 2005 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 A sharp fall in U.S. Treasurys sparked fresh selling at the longer end of Singapore's bond market Thursday, driving the benchmark 10-year bond yield to a five-month high. The 10-year bond opened at a yield of 3.22% and rose to 3.24% by 0400 GMT, up from 3.18% Wednesday. The market reacted to selling in the U.S., where Treasurys sank to new session lows overnight, sending the 10-year U.S. yield above 4.50% for the first time since July. The selling shows the city-state is facing a double-edged sword - the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has been on a tightening trend since October to contain inflation, while yields on the U.S. Treasurys are moving higher at a faster pace. In order to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar competitive, the MAS has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent a sharp fall in the U.S. dollar. Even on Thursday, suspected MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the U.S. dollar to rebound from a new six-year low of 1.6160 in early trade. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment. However, at the same time, MAS is also sterilizing surplus liquidity through the sale of domestic securities which, in fact, is leading to funds outflow and higher rates, said Thomas Lam Tai Loong, treasury economist at United Overseas Bank. He said the shorter end of the yield curve is reacting to the MAS action and rising U.S. rates - which have been firming up since the start of the year. The longer-dated bonds may still have support at 3.25%, said one dealer at a local bank, but other analysts said the bearish sentiment for the bond market is far from over. "For the 10-year Treasurys, our year-end forecast is 5.6%, so we should expect the Singapore bond yield to hit 3.6% by the end of the year," said Simon Klassen, fixed-income research analyst at JP Morgan. However, an expected rise in yields won't force companies to "to rush to the doors" for more corporate bond issuances because they have already covered themselves last year, he said. Foreign companies issued S$3.42 billion in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds in 2004, almost double the amount from 2003.  \par \par \uc2 -By Saeed Azhar, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415 4157; saeed.azhar@dowjones.com -Edited by Lena Lee  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020050310e139000dj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
