\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Down Vs US Dlr Late On Slew Of Factors  \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 08:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar slid late Friday in Asia on a slew of factors - higher oil prices, expectations for a strong U.S. February employment report and rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Prices on \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds fell moderately on fears of a stronger-than-expected U.S. nonfarm payrolls figure, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6327, up from S$1.6273 late Thursday. It traded between S$1.6277 and S$1.6334 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Late Thursday's spike in crude oil prices above US$55 a barrel on worries over a precarious global oil supply and demand balance in 2005 prompted some U.S. dollar short-covering against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in Friday's Asian session.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the surge in crude oil prices sparked concerns that they could hurt economic growth among Asian nations, which are major oil importers.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, prospects for a strong U.S. jobs figure were bolstered by the Institute for Supply Management's nonmanufacturing index issued late Thursday, which showed its employment index leaped to 59.6 in February from 52.2 in January. Readings above 50 in the ISM data point to growth.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Labor Department is due to issue the February jobs report at 1330 GMT. The median estimate of economists surveyed Monday by Dow Jones Newswires is for an increase of 220,000 in payrolls for February.  \par \par \uc2 Together with rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS to limit the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's downside against its Singaporean counterpart in the Asian session, the U.S. unit eventually rose as high as S$1.6334 in the afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar then consolidated with a firm bias in the low-S$1.6300s for the rest of the session, and technical analysis suggests the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue to edge higher against the local dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But, S$1.6330 is strong resistance, and this level is unlikely to break so easily, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Latest       Previous \par Overnight rate       1.8125%      1.7500% \par Two-year bond yield  2.11%        2.08% \par 10-year bond yield   3.22%        3.15% \par Two-year swap offer  2.36%        2.32% \par 10-year swap offer   3.45%        3.39% \par 2-10-yr swap spread  109 BPS      107 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Costas Paris [ 04-03-05 0718GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050304e1340008b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
