\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Up Late On Mild Yen Strength; Bonds Slip  \b0\par\par\uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2005\par 08:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was stronger late Tuesday in Asia on mild, broad-based strength in the yen, although the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's upside was curbed by fears of potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Prices on government bonds slipped after a fall in U.S. Treasurys late Monday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6247, down from S$1.6258 late Monday. It traded between S$1.6216 and S$1.6261 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 After falling to a six-year low of S$1.6214 late Monday in New York, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened Tuesday's Asian session on a negative note against the local dollar, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Despite light short-covering led by a U.S. bank that lifted the U.S. dollar as high as S$1.6261 early in the session, the U.S. unit was weighed down against the local dollar on the yen's slight rise against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the U.S. unit was at Y104.48, down from Y104.52 Monday in New York. The euro was at Y137.93, down from Y138.42 Monday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said yen-bullish sentiment continued to bolster the Singaporean and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Tuesday's Asian session after strong Japanese economic data were released Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Monday, the Japanese government said the nation's industrial production rose 2.1% in January from December, reversing the revised 0.8% fall of the previous month and beating market expectations for a 1.6% rise. Japan's overall retail sales rose 2.2% in January, against expectations of 0.3%.  \par \par \uc2 However, the U.S. dollar managed to hold above the six-year low of S$1.6214, given concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem any sharp fall below the psychologically important S$1.6200, a level which hasn't been broken since early November 1998.  \par \par \uc2 According to technical analysis, the U.S. dollar's downward momentum against its Singaporean counterpart is strong and there are no signs of stabilization just yet, United Overseas Bank said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 'It looks like a further down-move is likely from here. Only a move above S$1.6275 will indicate that we have seen a temporary short-term low,' UOB said.  \par \par \uc2 As a short-term trading strategy, UOB recommends selling the U.S. dollar at S$1.6250 and placing stop-loss orders above S$1.6275 in anticipation of a possible fall to S$1.6180.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Singapore government securities rose across the curve, in the wake of a sharp rise in U.S. Treasury yields late Monday in New York, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But trading flows in the local bond market were relatively thin ahead of this week's slew of U.S. economic data, including the 1500 GMT release of the Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing index for February.  \par \par \uc2 Economists expect the ISM manufacturing index to move up to 57.0 in February from 56.4 in January.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                       Latest       Previous \par Overnight rate       1.8750%      2.0000% \par Two-year bond yield  2.03%        2.02% \par 10-year bond yield   3.06%        3.05% \par Two-year swap offer  2.27%        2.27% \par 10-year swap offer   3.32%        3.30% \par 2-10-yr swap spread  105 BPS      103 BPs \par  \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lena Lee  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050301e131004xt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
