\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Falls Late On Suspected MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Februar 2005\par 08:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell against its U.S. counterpart late Friday in Asia, after apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to absorb heavy selling by U.S. investment banks.  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds were weaker across the board, especially for short-dated securities, resulting in curve flattening, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0649 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6329, up from S$1.6277 late Thursday. It traded between S$1.6284 and S$1.6332 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  kicked off Friday's Asian session on a positive note against the local dollar after broad-based U.S. dollar strength on the back of a climb in U.S. Treasury yields and U.S. equities late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 But U.S. investment banks soon sold the U.S. dollar down to as low as S$1.6284 early in the session, supposedly to bet on a renewed decline of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against its Singaporean counterpart in the near term, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 In an apparent effort to stem the U.S. unit's slide against the local dollar, the MAS was rumored to have bought the U.S. dollar around the intraday low of S$1.6284, thereby lifting the U.S. unit to the S$1.6330 level in the afternoon, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation that Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could continue to curb an excessive appreciation of the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  put a floor under the U.S. dollar in the S$1.6330 area for the rest of the Asian session.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also cited the 1330 GMT release of the U.S.'s preliminary gross domestic product data for the fourth quarter as another factor providing support for the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. GDP growth in the fourth quarter is expected to be revised upward, reflecting a narrowing in the trade deficit, among other things. The median estimate of 20 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for growth to be revised up to a 3.8% annual rate from the 3.1% rate in the advance report.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Singapore government bonds rose, particularly at the front end, leading to flattening of the yield curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 A flattening of the U.S. Treasurys yield curve late Thursday and a rise in Singapore's short-term interbank rates Friday boosted yields on short-dated securities.  \par \par \uc2 Since the start of this year, the three-month Singapore interbank offered rate, or Sibor, has risen to 1.87% from 1.50%, Standard Chartered Bank said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 While seasonally tight liquidity conditions around the Lunar New Year holidays earlier this month resulted in higher interbank rates, Standard Chartered said the fact that this rise in Singapore interest rates isn't abating is a potential concern and could quicken economic growth deceleration.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Latest       Previous \par Overnight rate       2.0000%      1.8750% \par Two-year bond yield  1.90%        1.86% \par 10-year bond yield   3.04%        3.03% \par Two-year swap offer  2.19%        2.10% \par 10-year swap offer   3.29%        3.24% \par 2-10-yr swap spread  110 BPS      114 BPs \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site:  \f28 \par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 6415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lena Lee [ 25-02-05 0702GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1163 1859  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020050225e12p00076\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
