\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dollar Up Late On Lingering US Dlr Bearishness  \b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2005\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar strengthened late Thursday in Asia on lingering bearish sentiment toward the U.S. dollar, despite suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government bonds were stronger after the MAS auctioned S$1.2 billion worth of 10-year government bonds, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0705 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6277, down from S$1.6295 late Wednesday. It traded between S$1.6263 and S$1.6298 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar came under downward pressure against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in the session as speculators sought to test the U.S. unit's one-month low of S$1.6250 set on Tuesday, following disappointing U.S. data and a lack of any positive surprises in the minutes from the Federal Reserve Open Market Committee's Feb. 1-2 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Underpinning bearish sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was Wednesday's U.S. data showing slower-than-expected growth in consumer prices in January. The U.S. government reported that the consumer price index rose 0.1% in January, and that core consumer prices increased 0.2%.  \par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires had forecast a 0.2% rise in both the overall index and the core component, which strips out food and energy prices.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the minutes from the FOMC's meeting, released Wednesday, also contributed to the U.S. dollar's weakness against the local dollar, as Fed policymakers said they see little evidence that the large U.S. trade deficit will narrow.  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. dollar slid as low as S$1.6263 in the morning, it soon found support around that level on suspected MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at slowing the U.S. unit's fall against its Singaporean counterpart, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar consolidated with a steady bias in the high-S$1.6200s for the rest of the session, bolstered by expectations that the MAS wouldn't hesitate to step back into the market to support the U.S. unit against the local dollar, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 Despite its oversold condition, however, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to form a short-term base against the Singapore dollar, based on technical analysis, United Overseas Bank said in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 "Any recovery is expected to be short-lived and another exhaustive down-move toward the next support at S$1.6230 is expected," said UOB.  \par \par \uc2 "Only a move above S$1.6340 will indicate that a stronger recovery is on the way," it said.  \par \par \uc2 The yield curve of Singapore government bonds flattened after the MAS's auction of S$1.2 billion in 10-year government bonds appeared to have been well-received by the local market, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The MAS sold S$1.2 billion in 10-year bonds at a cutoff yield of 3.00%. Bids totaled S$2.7 billion and the average yield was 2.97%, according to data posted on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Web site.  \par \par \uc2 A total S$94.1 million of the 10-year issue was allotted to noncompetitive applications, none of it to the MAS.  \par \par \uc2 The bonds, which will be issued March 1, are a reopening of an existing paper that matures July 1, 2014.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                      Latest       Pr