\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dlr Steady Late As Yuan Talk Continues To Shift  \b0\par\par\uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2005\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was relatively steady against its U.S. counterpart in Asia late Friday as speculation over a possible move by China to a flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime continued to swing back and forth.  \par \par \uc2 The yield curve of Singapore government bonds steepened, in line with Thursday's steepening of the U.S. Treasury yield curve, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0630 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6341, almost unchanged from S$1.6344 late in Asia Thursday. It traded between S$1.6278 and S$1.6347 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the Feb. 4-5 meeting of finance officials from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations, expectations that China might adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have seesawed almost every day as an increasing number of Chinese officials join the chorus of commenting on the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 People's Bank of China's monetary policy committee member Yu Yongding said Thursday that now is the time for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to revalue the Chinese yuan.  \par \par \uc2 'Now is the time to revalue,' Yu told journalists at the World Economic Forum after a dinner discussion on the dollar's weakness. 'We need more flexibility. That means revaluation.'  \par \par \uc2 Yu's remarks prompted speculators to dump U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars, pushing the U.S. unit as low as S$1.6278 early in Friday's Asian session, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon doused speculation of an imminent revaluation of the yuan, saying Friday that Yu's comments don't represent Beijing's view on the matter.  \par \par \uc2 '(Yu Yongding's) remarks are only his personal view, and the opinions of an academic. They do not represent the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy,' an official in the PBOC's information office told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Yu is an academic adviser to the PBOC's monetary policy committee. Though he isn't a government official, Yu advises the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its policies.  \par \par \uc2 The PBOC's clarification of Yu's comments, along with suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , boosted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as high as S$1.6347 in the morning, traders said, adding that the MAS was rumored to have bought a small amount of U.S. dollars around the S$1.6310 level.  \par \par \uc2 The rest of Friday's global session could see more volatile trading in the U.S. dollar against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the Singapore dollar, especially given the 1330 GMT release of U.S. advance fourth-quarter gross domestic product data.  \par \par \uc2 The median estimate of 20 economists surveyed Monday by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC is for a 3.6% annual rate of GDP growth with a 2.0% rate of increase for the chain-weighted price index.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Latest   Previous \par Overnight rate       1.5000%  1.6250% \par Two-year bond yield  1.79%    1.80% \par 10-year bond yield   2.92%    2.90% \par Two-year swap offer  1.97%    1.95% \par 10-year swap offer   3.14%    3.09% \par 2-10-yr swap spread  117 