\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Down Late On Suspected MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 707 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2005\par 08:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2005, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar fell moderately in Asia late Thursday as suspected U.S. dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore overwhelmed heavy selling of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by U.S. hedge funds.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on Singapore government bonds were lower in the front end than in the rest of the curve, leading to curve steepening, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0645 GMT, the U.S. unit was at S$1.6380, up from S$1.6335 late in Asia Wednesday. It traded between S$1.6299 and S$1.6392 intraday.  \par \par \uc2 Increased speculation that China may revalue the yuan next month and renewed expectations that Malaysia could change the ringgit peg this year prompted U.S. funds to sell the U.S. dollar down to as low as S$1.6299 early in the session, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation on the revaluation of the yuan was fueled by a news article citing a report by consultancy firm Medley Global Advisors that China might revalue the yuan by up to 5% after the weeklong Lunar New Year holiday, which starts Feb. 9, dealers added.  \par \par \uc2 Recently, U.S. and European policy-makers have increasingly called on Asian nations, and China in particular, to share more of the burden of the U.S. dollar's long-term weakness, sparking expectations that the Group of Seven major industrialized nations will pressure China to shift to a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime at the G7 meeting of finance officials Feb. 4-5.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, Malaysia's former Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad was quoted by national news agency Bernama as saying late Wednesday that Malaysia's ringgit peg, which has been in place for more than six years, should be reviewed as the weak U.S. dollar has made imports more expensive than they should be.  \par \par \uc2 The peg of 3.80 ringgit to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has served the economy well since its introduction in September 1998, although some analysts say the U.S. dollar's sharp fall in recent months and expectations of a revaluation of the yuan imply the ringgit is undervalued.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday, Malaysian Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Zeti Akhtar Aziz reassured markets that no change to the ringgit peg was imminent.  \par \par \uc2 Although U.S. funds used the two news reports as reasons to sell U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars throughout the session, traders said the MAS apparently stepped into the market at least twice to prop up the ailing U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the local dollar, eventually lifting the U.S. unit as high as S$1.6392 in the afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 According to technical analysis, the U.S. dollar's sharp recovery off the low of S$1.6275 suggests the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely continue edging higher for the global day, said United Overseas Bank in a research report.  \par \par \uc2 But UOB added that the overall outlook for the U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar pair is still very mixed and any further gains will likely find resistance at S$1.6400 difficult to break.  \par \par \uc2 The yield curve of Singapore government bonds, meanwhile, bucked the flattening of the U.S. Treasurys yield curve in New York late Wednesday, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The benign U.S. consumer price index report released Wednesday may have provided the local bond market with the incentive to buy short-dated securities. The December CPI fell 0.1%, slightly below economists' expectations for overall prices to be unchanged. The core index, which excludes food and energy, rose by 0.2%, as expected.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the overnight interbank rate, yields on Singapore government bonds and rates on interest-rate swaps:  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                      Latest   Previous \par Overnight rate       1.2500%  0.8125% \par Two-year bond yield  1.64%    1.67% \par 10-year bond yield   2.88%    2.83% \par Two-year swap offer  1.90%    1.92% \par 10-year swap offer   3.05%    3.03% \par 2-10-yr swap spread  115 BPs  111 BPs \par  \par   Historical bond yields and final prices can be viewed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's bond Web site: \par  \par     \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sgs.gov.sg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.sgs.gov.sg }}}\uc2 \f2  \par  \par -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-415-4150; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Lena Lee \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020050120e11k004xv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
